{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Remember the sabbath day, to sanctify it.</b> After He commanded belief of the heart in the distinctive Name, and that we not exchange it for another, and commanded honoring the mention of His name, He also commanded that we make in it a sign and a remembrance in the days, to sanctify the seventh day, which is the sabbath, to make known that He, exalted be He, renewed the world and created everything something from nothing." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): זכור את יום השבת — that we always remember, every day, the sabbath day, so that we do not forget it and so that it not become confused for us with the other days. For when we remember it constantly, we remember the act of Creation at all times, and this is a great principle of faith, for from the renewal of the world the matter of providence and prophecy and the truth of punishment and reward become clear. And it says לקדשו — that we recall its holiness, so that it be holy in our eyes, and that we free our thoughts on it from bodily occupations and engage in it in the ways of the blessed Name, which are the occupations of the soul. For we are obligated to go to the prophets and to the sages of every generation to seek Torah from their mouths, to hear the words of the blessed Name, as the matter that our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (II Kings 4:23): ״מדוע את הולכת אליו היום לא חודש ולא שבת״ — from which it follows that on the new moon and the sabbath one must go. And so David said (Psalms 119:97): ״כל היום היא שיחתי״ — it should have said 'in every day,' but he alludes to the particular day, which is the sabbath, on which he delighted in the occupation of Torah the entire day. And because of the holiness of the sabbath day the prophet called it עונג (delight), as it is said (Isaiah 58:13): ״וקראת לשבת עונג״ — that is, delight of the soul. For the discerning and those who fear HaShem delight in it in the occupations of the soul, and their rational soul takes pleasure in intellectual delight and yearns for the attainment of the blessed Name with the yearning of the thirsty for water, as the manner of what is written (Psalms 42:3): ״צמאה נפשי לאלהים לאל חי״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: all who observe the sabbath — all their sins are forgiven, even if he was an idolater like the generation of Enosh, his sins are forgiven. As it is said (Isaiah 56:2): ״אשרי אנוש יעשה זאת וגו׳ שומר שבת מחללו״ — do not read מחללו but rather מחול לו, 'it is forgiven him.' There is an incident involving the wicked Turnus Rufus who encountered R. Akiva on the sabbath. He said to him: 'How is this day different from other days?' R. Akiva answered him: 'And how is this man different from other men?' He said to him: 'My lord's gazelle.' R. Akiva said to him: 'The sabbath too is my Lord's gazelle' — just as he wished to honor you, so the King, King of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He, wished that the sabbath be honored. He said to him: 'If so, why does your Elohim do work on the sabbath?' He said to him: 'And what work does He do?' He said to him: 'In the same way that He does on weekdays, He does on the sabbath — He makes the wind blow and brings down the rain, raises clouds, causes the sun and moon to shine, and ripens fruits.' R. Akiva said to him: 'I know that you are versed in the Torah of the Hebrews. Two men who dwell in one courtyard — this one sets an עירוב for that one and that one sets an עירוב for this one, and they are permitted to carry on the sabbath. But a single individual dwelling in one large courtyard, even as large as Antioch, may carry in his courtyard, because there is no other domain with him. And the Holy One, blessed be He — the heavens are His throne and the earth is His footstool, the whole earth is full of His glory; there is no other domain with Him to carry in His world.' And not only that, but the eaters of the manna testify concerning Him: all the days of the week it descended, and on the sabbath it did not descend. And not only that — the river Sambatyon testifies: all six days it flows, and on the sabbath it does not flow. He said to him: 'Leave the manna, which was not in our days, and the river Sambatyon I do not believe you about.' He said to him: 'Go to an ov and yidoni, all the days of the week they succeed in their craft, and on the sabbath they do not succeed. Go and check your father's grave and you will see — all six days smoke rises from his grave, and on the sabbath it does not rise. And the dead know that it is the sabbath, but the living do not observe it.' He went and checked his father's grave and saw that on the sabbath no smoke was rising. He said: 'Perhaps his sentence is completed.' He said to him: 'Tomorrow you will see.' On Sunday he saw the smoke rising. He went back and performed sorcery and raised him from the grave. He said to him: 'In your lifetime you did not observe the sabbath; in your death you observe it. From when did you become a Jew?' He said to him: 'My son, everyone who does not observe the sabbath among you, as is your law, comes here and observes it against his will.' He said to him: 'And what work is done among you on the weekdays?' He said to him: 'All the days of the week we are judged, and on the sabbath we rest. And on the eve of the sabbath a bat kol proclaims and says: Leave them, the wicked, and let them rest. And an angel whose name is דומה is appointed over us and judges us all the days. And at the departure of the sabbath, when the orders are completed, that angel cries out and says: Return, wicked, to Gehinnom, for Israel have already completed their orders.' End of the Midrash. And this is why all Israel are accustomed not to rush through ויהי נועם and the Order of Qedushah, but rather to wait until it is quite dark, and to recite them slowly. And for this reason our Rabbis, of blessed memory, forbade drinking water on the eve of the sabbath at twilight — as is found in the Midrash: whoever drinks water on the eve of the sabbath at twilight is robbing the dead. Their intention in this was to fix in the soul the belief that the wicked of Israel have rest in the sabbath in Gehinnom. And when they go out on the eve of the sabbath they drink and bathe and cool themselves in water, so that one who drinks water at that hour — they needed to say that he is robbing them of it, and they made the one who drinks a robber and a denier of faith. And for this reason we do not recite on the evening of the sabbath והוא רחום, because three angels — namely משחית, אף, and חמה — who are appointed over the wicked in the suffering of Gehinnom have no authority on the sabbath, and in order to fix in the heart that the wicked have rest on the sabbath and that those appointed officers have no dominion over them on the sabbath. And all three are inscribed in the verse of והוא רחום. And this Midrash — I do not know where it is, but I have heard that it is found among our Rabbis, of blessed memory, in a book called Masekhet Gan Eden or Pirkei Gan Eden, similar to Pirkei Hekhalot and Pirkei Merkavot and Pirkei Derekh Eretz. And so it is written there in another version: whoever drinks water on the sabbath between Minhah and Maarit within the year that a relative of his died — this one is robbing his dead. And there the reason is explained in those chapters: because the dead go out on the sabbath and drink water from the river that goes out from Eden, and therefore it is not fitting for him to rob his dead; but after a year, he has already accepted his judgment." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel), regarding the commandment of the sabbath. Know that this physical world is governed by light and darkness, day and night, so as not to burden creatures with toil always. Therefore He gave the day for the work of creatures, for in it man goes out to his labor and his work until evening, and the night for rest and sleep, for creatures need to rest from their toil and weariness in labor, and they also need sleep, which is a natural matter in man — there is no physical living being in the world that does not sleep, even the gnat, for it cannot endure without sleep. And if the world were governed by light alone, the creatures' toil would be continuous over unbroken time and the world could not endure. So too the commandment of the sabbath: not to burden creatures with constant toil on all the days, but rather that they have a day of rest in the week, on which they rest from labor and from bodily toil. And from this the seventh part of a person's life will be in rest and tranquility. And the reason it is the seventh day and not the fifth or the sixth is because seven is the middle encompassing among the encompassings, and many commandments in the Torah revolve around this number's pattern. And even though the sabbath is received [by tradition] and the roots of Torah and faith are built upon it, still, this may be said of it by way of reason (sekhel)." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: זכור את יום השבת. Know that the sabbath is the final sefirah, for so the sabbath day is the last, the completion of all the work of Creation, and it is called Kneset Yisrael. And in this manner our Rabbis, of blessed memory, alluded and said: 'Kneset Yisrael is the mate of the sabbath.' And from this they called the sabbath day מטרונא — as they expounded concerning the matter of an animal offering: עד שתראה פני מטרונא תחלה — and the sabbath is called ברית, just as He mentioned in the sign of circumcision (Genesis 17:10): ״זאת בריתי״, and so in the sign of the rainbow (ibid. 9): ״זאת אות הברית״. And the reason Scripture mentioned here זכור — the intent is upon the great sabbath, which is the attribute of Yesod, from which the 248 positive commandments flow, alluded to in it by זכו״ר י״ה, and it is the sabbath day. And when He mentioned in Mishneh Torah שמור, the intent is in the sabbath called מקדש, which is Hod, from which the 365 negative commandments flow, in which women are obligated in all of them. And this is the secret of (Leviticus 26:2): ״את שבתותי תשמרו ומקדשי תיראו״. And from this you will find in Jeremiah (Jeremiah 17:24): ״לבלתי עשות בו כל מלאכה״ — there it is written with a keri. And the reason it says ״ויום השביעי שבת לה׳ אלהיך״ — it explains that the sabbath is on the seventh day, for the seventh day is יסוד עולם, which is the seventh sefirah, and the sabbath is in it, for all is in all. And so we find in the section of כי תשא (Exodus 31:15): ״וביום השביעי שבת שבתון קדש לה׳״, and afterward it is written: ״לעשות את יום השבת ברית עולם״. He called the seventh day, which is יסוד עולם, שבת שבתון קדש לה׳, and He called the sabbath that receives from it ברית. Once you understand all this, you need not wonder why the Sages, of blessed memory, called the daytime sanctification קדושא רבא, unlike at night — for the day corresponds to זכור and the night corresponds to שמור, and this is clear. And for this intention the Prophet needed to call the sabbath עונג, as it is written (Isaiah 58:13): ״וקראת לשבת עונג״, for it is the delight of souls. And so David, king of Israel, of blessed memory, said (Psalms 42:3): ״צמאה נפשי לאלהים לאל חי״ — for everything thirsts for its root, which it is destined to return to, and it is the river that waters the garden, which is Yesod of the sabbath and its root. And this is the word ענ״ג: עדן נהר גן, from what is written (Genesis 2:10): ״ונהר יוצא מעדן להשקות את הגן״. And therefore the sabbath is called עונג, for it is the delight of the supernal ones and the lower ones."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :register {:flags {2 ["Lord"]}}, :notes {2 "R. Bahya's citation '(II Kings 4:23)' precedes the verse quotation מדוע את הולכת אליו היום לא חודש ולא שבת; the Hebrew text gives the citation in the form (מלכים ב ד:כ״ג) with the parenthetical gloss, rendered here as it stands. The abbreviation רז״ל rendered as 'our Rabbis, of blessed memory.' The bat kol passage and the והוא רחום explanation are transmitted as R. Bahya wrote them; the three angel-names משחית אף חמה are left as given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:10.011467389Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}